No; we had been discussing this in the context of an _80_% chance in a
single magic lamp. We had *not* been talking about a random WoW (0:3).

In case of such an abundance of random wishes[*] with one single item
I'd immediately wish for at least GDSM. Then I'd head to Sokoban which
is on a quite shallow level and can usually be reached undisturbed,
specifically with that AC from the GDSM. If Sokoban has the amulet I
can save another wish for this, but judge whether I might need speed
boots. Otherwise the next wish would be for reflection. Etc. This only
as example; I vary my decisions based on actual role, race, findings,
and dungeon situation. For example if I find the source of wish(es) on
a lower level, then I'd ask myself whether my character is too fragile
or robust enough to continue to see whether there are dragons nearby;
this can be Ludios - you should have reflection for that! -, or the
Castle, or (in case of that role) the healer quest. So I continuously
value the game and chances for the best outcome - e.g. an invisible
hostile golden naga will make it necessary to get MR quickly, and an
available wish applied. I try to do not only short term decisions. If
you find that wrong... *shrug* - it seems to work well for me.

The more interesting question that was behind my stated strategy is the
case of a single wish from a magic lamp. Here my personal experience lead
me to the following strategy; instead of relying on the _80_% chance keep
the lamp. The lamp provides short term light for thorough exploration of
the mines and long term light to finish the game. In case where I need a
wish to circumvent or solve some tight situation (I gave an example above)
I'd try for the 80%. In the past I had bad experience with failures, so my
mileage may vary from yours, when judging about what's best short and long
term.

A single unexpected random wish is of course no issue; wish for whatever
you are currently lacking, probably biased by the fator I mentioned above
(role, race, etc.).

This all works quite well for me.

The dominant factor, though, is anyway that random early wishes are rare.
But the general strategy outlined above is applicable on every stage, not
only early game.

